{"results": [{"id": "FAM:SF07", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF07 | Study family SF07: Sequential pair premium and defensive aftershock (8/8 PTs)\n- observed_research_question: Sequential pair premium and defensive aftershock (8/8 PTs)\n- auto_outcome_status: supported\n- observed_hypothesis: After the opponent runs play type X, the next offensive play type Y scores above Y's unconditional H2 baseline, and every PT has a defensive aftershock.\n- observed_result: [APPA-049] A2 aftershock 8/8 PTs replicate all-games and playoffs (CUT +0.119 n=21,398 / PO +0.131 n=1,145 through TRANSITION +0.135 n=26,262 / +0.126 n=1,529). Top pair premiums: PUTBACK->TRANSITION +0.084 n=565; CUT->P [cut; read_family for the full text]\n- observed_result: [APPS-037] All-games aftershock after success: CUT +0.119 (n=21398), DRIVE +0.123 (57621), PNR +0.127 (51194), POST_UP +0.128 (20619), PULL_UP +0.132 (26060), PUTBACK +0.130 (5888), SPOT_UP +0.128 (43857), TRANSITION +0. [cut; read_family for the full text]\n- auto_failure_mechanism: D_positive (no failure: positive result)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025", "score": null, "document": "# FAM:SF-CHT-025 | Study family SF-CHT-025: Play-type labeling audit: OTHER/DRIVE foul leakage\n- observed_research_question: Play-type labeling audit: OTHER/DRIVE foul leakage\n- auto_outcome_status: artifact\n- observed_hypothesis: Play-type PPP differences are real efficiency, not labeling/foul artifacts.\n- observed_result: OTHER: 36% end in fouls, PPP inflated (FOUL_BUCKET). DRIVE: 12pp below foul benchmark (FOUL_LEAK). PNR shows 22% foul rate. Only 335 of 50,668 possessions (0.7%) map to a real play type via initiation_type.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: C_artifact (apparent effect produced by a construction, selection or labelling flaw)\n- observed_failure_evidence: OTHER PPP inflated by 36% foul endings; DRIVE 12pp below foul benchmark; initiation_type maps 0.7%.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 50,668 possessions in the initiation_type check\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_method: Per-PT diagnostic of foul-ending share vs PPP; initiation_type mapping.\n- observed_verdict: artifact_found"}], "n_results": 2}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "play-type labeling",
    "AVOID labels",
    "opponent next-possession points per possession",
    "own-sequence lagged outcomes",
    "effect size of play type on subsequent opponent PPP"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "Confounding in next-possession outcomes after play types",
    "Measurement reliability of derived play-type labels",
    "Base rate and sample size for infrequent play types",
    "Autocorrelation of possession outcomes affecting labeling"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF07",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Found that after an offensive possession of any play type succeeds, the opponent's next possession scores significantly below baseline (defensive aftershock of ~0.12\u20130.14 PPP across 8 play types, all-games n=458,604). Pair-specific sequential premiums exist (e.g., PUTBACK\u2192TRANSITION +0.084). Own-sequence lags were exploratory (~4% of pairs). Effect is uniform enough that it may reflect a generic dead-ball/score effect rather than play-type-specific disruption."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Audit revealed that play-type PPP differences are artifacts of foul leakage: OTHER type has 36% foul endings inflating PPP, DRIVE shows foul leakage 12pp below benchmark, and only 0.7% of possessions map to a real play type via initiation_type."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
      "what_failed": "Using derived play-type PPP as a measure of true efficiency due to labeling and foul artifacts",
      "why": "labeling flaw (C_artifact): foul endings cause upward bias in OTHER and downward bias in DRIVE; initiation_type mapping covers only 0.7%"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Opponent next-possession PPP after a successful play-type possession is reliably lower than baseline (defensive aftershock), consistent across all 8 play types.",
    "Sequential pair premiums exist for specific play-type transitions.",
    "The own-sequence lag effect on next possession PPP after a play type is small and not well-characterized."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether opponent next-possession PPP after a play type (including failed possessions) differs across play types enough to drive AVOID labels.",
    "Whether own-sequence lags produce practically meaningful differences in PPP.",
    "Whether the apparent aftershock is play-type-specific or a generic score/possession-type effect.",
    "The magnitude and robustness of effects when labeling artifacts are corrected."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question can be answered with the available data if the analysis distinguishes successful and failed possessions, controls for game-state confounders, and uses initiation-validated play-type labels.",
    "The uniform aftershock suggests that simply using opponent next-possession PPP may not differentiate play types for AVOID recommendations; play-type-specific heterogeneity must be explicitly tested.",
    "Labeling flaws shown in SF-CHT-025 (foul leakage, low initiation mapping) could bias the estimated effects and must be mitigated, e.g., by restricting to possessions with reliable play-type assignment or using initiation_type."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "Whether the small sample of rare play types (PUTBACK n=5,888) provides enough power to detect play-type-specific differences after adjusting for confounders.",
    "The extent to which the AVOID label threshold can be operationalized from opponent next-possession PPP without circularity.",
    "How much the labeling improvements since SF-CHT-025 have reduced the artifact rate."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "SF07 shows that a signal exists in opponent next-possession PPP, suggesting the question is not untestable, but the uniform nature of the aftershock and the labeling artifact from SF-CHT-025 indicate that a straightforward analysis would risk producing an artifact. Therefore the design must incorporate play-type-specific heterogeneity tests and labeling quality checks, changing from a simple comparison to a controlled, interaction-focused design."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.75,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play-type labels contain artifacts (foul leakage, low initiation mapping) that could bias measured opponent next PPP and own-sequence lags."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Rare play types (e.g., PUTBACK) may have insufficient sample for conditional analysis after splitting by success/failure and context."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF07",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-025"
    ],
    "summary": "Prior work found a uniform defensive aftershock after successful play-type possessions (SF07) and identified artifacts in play-type labeling (SF-CHT-025), leaving open the question of play-type-specific effects large enough to drive AVOID labels."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Play-type labeling flaws (foul leakage, low initiation mapping) may bias the measured opponent next PPP and own-sequence lags, as shown in SF-CHT-025."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Team quality, game score, period, opponent defensive strength, and home/away could confound the relationship between play type and next-possession PPP."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Some play types (e.g., PUTBACK, POST_UP) have low frequencies; sub-group analyses by success and context may be underpowered."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Comparing 8 play types and multiple pair transitions inflates the risk of false positives."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If AVOID labels are defined from the same opponent next PPP outcomes, the analysis becomes circular; a pre-registered threshold is needed."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.3,
    "uncertain": 0.1
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of running a given play type X (versus average of all other play types) on opponent next-possession points per possession, adjusted for success/failure, score margin, period, and opponent defensive strength; and the effect of own-sequence lags (X vs other) on own next-possession PPP.",
    "unit": "possession",
    "sample": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season and playoffs, excluding garbage time (score differential >25 in 4th quarter, >20 in last 2 min of 4th) and possessions with ambiguous play-type labels (OTHER type with >30% foul endings, or missing initiation_type mapping).",
    "power": "Minimum detectable effect size of 0.03 PPP per play type with 80% power using a multi-level model; all four seasons provide >500,000 opponent-next possessions.",
    "controls": "Fixed effects: play type (reference SPOT_UP), success indicator (made basket or shooting foul drawn), score margin (linear and quadratic), period, home/away, opponent defensive rating bin (quartiles), and team-season random intercepts. Sensitivity analysis with game fixed effects.",
    "falsification": "Test for play-type × success interaction; if the uniform aftershock replicates with no play-type interaction, the claim of PT-specific AVOID drivers is rejected. Permute play-type labels to generate placebo distribution.",
    "multiplicity": "Bonferroni-Holm correction across 8 play-type comparisons and 4 own-lag comparisons; report familywise error rate.",
    "decision_rule": "Conclude a play type drives AVOID if its opponent next-possession PPP is significantly higher than the PT-average PPP by at least 0.05 PPP after adjustment, and the PT×success interaction is not significant (i.e., effect not solely due to success).",
    "data_version": "Per-game files as of 2026-06-19; play-type labels from the Neo4j graph database filtered to possessions where initiation_type mapping coverage exceeds 80% and OTHER type excluded."
  }
}}

